## Quality Check Rules

**All quality verification is done locally. CI is for build confirmation only.**

### Flow

```
Harness-only diff? -> skip quality-check entirely (the gate exempts it;
                      gate-parameter changes are NOT exempt - see Merge Gate)
    |
Step 1: Change area + risk level detection (git diff; risk levels per quality policy §1)
    |
Step 2: Build + static checks -> Step 3: tests (High/Medium: cross-check the test-design
        memo). If failures remain after the one fix pass, the cycle ends here (no
        personas) and the next cycle starts
    |
Step 4: Review cycle (personas selected by change content - see the quality-check
        skill's applicability table; normally 1 cycle; cycles 2+ verify fixes with
        only the personas that raised findings; the QA persona works falsification-first)
    |
Step 5: Additional-test proposal (test-recommendation skill: heuristics recommend
        mutation/E2E runs with rationale, the user decides, approved runs execute;
        declines are recorded and never block - except an executed E2E that fails
        must be fixed)
    |
Step 6: Report data saved -> .quality-check-passed (commit-bound JSON) created -> merge allowed
```

Thresholds, cycle caps and time budgets are defined in `documents/development/quality-policy.md` (§1 risk levels, §2 gate matrix, §5 loop protection). Do not restate the numbers here - read them from the policy.

### Merge Gate

The hook is a static classifier of the command line - it never resolves what actually lands on main; it only stops direct pushes/merges into main (or the trunk equivalent).

- **Target**: `gh pr merge`, `gh api ...pulls/<n>/merge`, `git merge` / `git pull` / `git rebase` on main, and a `git push` whose destination refspec matches `main` / `master` exactly. **Pushes to feature branches are NOT gated** - push freely at any time.
- **Unconditional blocks** (no exemption, regardless of flag or branch): force/delete pushes and `+refspec` / `--mirror` / `--all`; another mover (checkout/switch/commit/reset/rebase/fetch/branch -f/update-ref/cherry-pick/stash pop) on the same line as a gated call; `-C` / `--git-dir` / `--work-tree` / `--namespace` / `-c` / `--config-env` / a `GIT_*=` assignment / `cd` or `pushd` sharing the line with a gated call; shell-expansion characters in a ref/remote/path value; redirection immediately before a gated word; more than one gated operation on the same line; and a push refspec of the form `<x>:main` where `<x>` is neither the current branch nor `HEAD`. Split these into separate commands and run plain git from the target directory.
- **What passes**: `.quality-check-passed` (commit-bound JSON `{branch, commit}` at the repo root, not consumed) has `commit` equal to `HEAD` (`branch` is diagnostic only), OR `commit` is an ancestor of `HEAD` and every file in `commit..HEAD` is a harness file with no control-plane touch and no `Quality Gate Overrides` / `mutation_budget_minutes` string change (see the carve-out below - validity is not parsed, a commented-out or fenced change still blocks). Separately, syncing main from its own remote with no other options - exactly `git pull`, `git pull origin main`, or `git merge origin/main` - needs no flag.
- Harness-only diffs (`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.cursorrules`, `.claude/**`, `.codex/**`, `.cursor/**`, `skills/project/**`, `skills/superpowers/**`, `.github/review-*.md`, `documents/development/coding-rules/**`) skip the gate and the quality check entirely. **Carve-out**: a diff that changes gate parameters - the `Quality Gate Overrides` keys below - is NOT exempt, even inside those paths. It must go through quality-check. The same applies to **gate control-plane files** - `skills/project/quality-check/**`, `skills/project/_schemas/**` and `skills/project/test-recommendation/**`, the `.claude/skills` and `.claude/hooks` trees (including the `.claude/skills` symlink node itself - `init` creates it as a link, and re-pointing that one path swaps the whole quality-check tree) and their `.codex/**` / `.cursor/**` copies, `.github/review-*.md`, the hook registration files (`.codex/hooks.json` and the `.claude` / `.cursor` equivalents), `.codex/config.toml` (whole-file: it can carry an inline `[hooks]` table, a `[features]` hooks kill-switch and `[[rules]]` deny decisions), the MCP registration files (`.claude/mcp.json` / `.codex/mcp.json` / `.cursor/mcp.json` - MCP server definitions register command execution, the same class as hooks), and the hook registration inside `.claude/settings.json` AND `.claude/settings.local.json` (Claude Code reads both, the latter at higher precedence): the `hooks` block, the `disableAllHooks` / `allowManagedHooksOnly` hook-disable keys, and the `permissions.deny` rule list (the deny layer backing the destructive-command guards; `permissions.allow` changes stay exempt) - unregistering or disabling the hook, or weakening the deny layer, disables protection just as surely as editing the hook does. Path matching is case-insensitive (`.claude/Hooks/...` is the same file as `.claude/hooks/...` on Windows/macOS). With in-development reviews consolidated into the merge gate, these must never change with zero review - the quality-gate hook enforces this carve-out and blocks such diffs with `Gate control-plane changed:`.
- **Fail-open** applies only to a malformed hook payload or a non-git directory; any other git failure with a gated candidate present blocks (stderr carries the reason).
- **Trunk names are fixed to `main` / `master`**; products using another trunk name are not gated by the hook and rely on the `permissions.deny` layer and convention.
- The hook evaluates in the session's working directory. Merging into a `main` checked out in a git worktree from another directory is outside its reach: run it from a session whose working directory is that worktree, or run quality-check and create the flag first.
- If main advanced independently after the flag was issued, the merged main (feature + main combined) is unverified: the merge may succeed but the push to main is blocked. Re-run `/quality-check` on the merged main, then push.

